    <section class="rd-section reveal">
      <header class="rd-section__head"><p class="rd-kicker">Before you buy</p><h2>Five checks that matter more than marketing copy</h2></header>
      <div class="rd-buying-grid">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>Toy, pet, project or companion?</h3><p>Bittle can delight a robotics hobbyist and disappoint somebody who wanted affection. The category name hides completely different jobs.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>How much setup?</h3><p>Remote-control toys start quickly. Programmable quadrupeds may need software, Wi-Fi, drivers, coding tools and patience.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>What works without an app?</h3><p>Connected features are useful, but buyers should know what remains if an app, account or cloud service changes.</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>Battery and charging</h3><p>Autonomous AI features consume more power. A docked home robot behaves very differently from a simple rechargeable toy.</p></article>
        <article><span>05</span><h3>Do not buy specs nobody will use</h3><p>ROS, LiDAR and computer vision are valuable only when the recipient genuinely wants to experiment with them.</p></article>
      </div>
